      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sits where Farm Roads 198 and 1335 cross, just six miles northeast of Cooper in Delta County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/lake-creek-tx",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the late 1800s Lake Creek thrived on cotton, operating several gins, general stores, and a Populist-era cooperative store.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Lake_Creek_Texas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 1873 blacksmith shop built by Thomas Wilson Stegall became the community’s early commercial hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/lake-creek-tx",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "After reopening in 1885, Lake Creek’s post office began daily mail delivery by 1888.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/lake-creek-tx",
